more than a million lined the streets to  celebrate the end of the war with  Germany but this year the 75th  anniversary those streets are set to be  empty events up and down the country  planned for months and years canceled  the ones who fought bravely on the  battlefields now trapped inside we've  locked down unlikely to end by Friday  there's little chance of parties like  these at the 70th anniversary in 2015  for the former head of the British Army  insists the country has a duty to  commemorate any way it can v-day is a  very special day and the 75th  anniversary of d-day is particularly  special when we cast our minds back to  the previous generation who fought so  valiantly for the peace and freedom of  Liberty that we take for granted today  it's really important that we  commemorate those who fell and celebrate  the service of those who saw us through  those difficult days of the Second World  War I hope you'll really celebrate the  e75 in the best way we can even though  we're still in this terrible lockdown  it means coming up with creative ways to  mark the anniversary at home for this  charity workshop in Kent veterans are  manning the machines creating special  perspex figurines of tommy soldiers the  aim for households up and down the  country to put them on window sills a  visual show of support for servicemen  and women past and present this is a  very emotional time for me  packing at tommy it it's not only  celebrating or should I say celebrating  the lives that gave us a freedom but  it's also a poignant moment because I  lost 56 comrades on the Sir Galahad when  I was in the Falklands so this is very  emotional  like many charities Royal British Legion  Industries is struggling during  coronavirus income has dropped by 70%  three-quarters of staff have been  furloughed the money from the figurines  will go to supporting veteran care homes  and those living alone during lockdown  people in the wider public are  understanding the sacrifices that the  services have given not only during the  footman first in the second world war  but also nowadays you see servicemen and  women on the front line doing testing  you see people here working and that  recognition and that unbroken line of  service to their country has actually  been a tremendous amount never more  evident than the now Colonel Tom Moore's  sponsored walk 75 years after serving in  Burma raising 30 million pounds for the  NHS this weekend was supposed to be a  celebration for World War two veterans  like Tom instead they find themselves  among the most at risk from a new enemy  the challenge for charities to find a  way for them to see how much their  sacrifices are still appreciated Sam  Holder Sky News
 

